    68	## 3. Recursion design
    69	
    70	### 3.1 Chain
    71	
    72	```
    73	owner run -> witness stream -> LEAF (sherlock-leaf/1, S steps, stmt/1 seven lanes)
    74	          -> NODE N (ivc.rs, self-recursive; [vk_N_repr, prog_d, init_d, cur_d, h, acc])
    75	          -> RUN DECIDER D (run_decider.rs; values [prog_d, init_d, fin_d, n_steps, result_be]; Blake2b)
    76	          -> SherlockRun.acceptRun(stmt, p, i, n, fin, r)  (Compact 0.36.0; decider VK installed)
    77	```
    78	
    79	Measured at `8fd34ed79` pins: one C6-shaped digest leaf with 96 private lanes (M2, not `standin.rs`) k=13, 2.55 s, 3,824 B; node k=20, 531,989 rows (7,701 over 2^19, 1.47%), ~50 s keygen, ~95 s/step, 26.2 GB; decider k=20, 833,235 rows, ~100 s, 70 lanes mutation-refused (`stagenet-gp-recursion.md:184-201`). These numbers are re-measured at the new pins (M2). The primary path (2026-11-08 (3)) is this chain with S-batched leaves and 2-3 parallel leaf workers proving ahead of the node chain under the 100 GB aggregate cap (`prover.md` F11: leaves are independent, so with P workers `T ~ max(N x t_node, N x t_leaf / P) + t_node + t_decider` and the wall is the node chain alone while `k_U <= 17`). The tree node of D13' is the measured alternative for the node layer; both are instances of the aggregation model of §3.6, so the statement, decider and `acceptRun` are the same.
