    27	## 1. Findings, ranked
    28	
    29	### F1. After S=8 the node is no longer the critical path; the universal leaf's k is
    30	`t_total(S) ≈ 293 × t_step + (293/S) × t_node + t_decider`. The node term shrinks with S,
    31	the leaf term does not: every owner step pays one universal step's rows regardless of S.
    32	So the single number that decides whether 2 h is reachable is `k_U`, the universal
    33	leaf's k at S=1 (M4, SRC-013: U if k_U ≤ 17). With `t_step = 0.09 ms × 2^k_U`:
    34	k_U=15 → 3 s; 16 → 6 s; 17 → 12 s; 18 → 24 s per step (upper bound 3.3× these).
    35	Measurement that decides: M4 row count and the C0 `synthesis` (phase 7) share of the leaf.
    36	
    37	### F2. S=8 alone brings fact(3) under 2 h on CPU if k_U ≤ 16 (ESTIMATE)
    38	37 leaves (36×8 + one chunk of 5, 3 padded slots = 1.0% waste). Leaf at k_U+3 = k19:
    39	47 s (0.09 ms/row) to 157 s (0.3 ms/row). Node 95 s unchanged.
    40	Serial: 37 × (95 + 47) + 100 = **5,354 s = 1.49 h** (k_U=16); 37 × (95 + 94) + 100 =
    41	7,093 s = 1.97 h (k_U=17, leaf at k20); fails at k_U=18 (S capped at 4 by k ≤ 20:
    42	74 × (95 + 94) + 100 = 14,086 s = 3.9 h). Memory: one prover at a time, ≤ 26 GB.
    43	
    44	### F3. The 1.5% at k=20 is inside the noise of the re-pin; measure before trimming
    45	The 531,989 rows were measured at circuits 7.2.4. The 7.3.0 release (what task 2.1
    46	pins) changes `as_public_input` to a canonical encoding for foreign-field elements and
    47	foreign Weierstrass points and makes `assign_as_public_input` run the full `assign`
    48	checks (#612, #598, `circuits/CHANGELOG.md:83-99`). The node carries two BLS12-381 points
    49	and their scalars in its public instance (`ivc.rs:187,370`; `inner.rs:38-49`), so the row
    50	count moves at 7.3.0, and more likely up than down (canonical-encoding checks add
    51	range comparisons; ESTIMATE +0.5k to +3k rows). Task 2.6 must print the row count
    52	(`cost_model`/dev row report) before anyone trims. See §2 for the trim list.
    53	
    54	### F4. The node's own proof format is free to change; only the decider's is not
    55	Stock Midnight verifies the decider proof (midnight-proofs 0.8.3 format, ledger 9.1.0.1).
    56	The leaf and node proofs are verified only by our circuits, but those circuits are built
    57	with the same `midnight-circuits` verifier gadget, which only verifies proofs of the
    58	`midnight-proofs` version it is compiled with. One workspace, one version: the pin is
    59	all-or-nothing. Consequence: the upstream `[Unreleased]` verifier-gadget row cuts (F9)
    60	are unusable until a ledger release pins a proofs > 0.8.3. GPU/CPU backend swaps (F5-F7)
    61	do not touch the format at all: an MSM or NTT computed elsewhere must give the same
    62	bytes, and that is checkable against the stock verifier we already run (A5/G10 pattern).
    63	
    64	### F5. GPU on this host: real but modest; the memory bus is shared with the CPU
    65	GB10 has 6,144 cores (an RTX 5070's count), FP32 31 TFLOPS, 273 GB/s unified memory.
    66	Field arithmetic runs on the int32 pipeline and NTT is bandwidth-bound; ZKProphet
    67	(arXiv 2509.22684) reports that once MSM is optimised NTT is up to 90% of GPU proof
    68	latency and that performance is capped by integer units, not FLOPS
    69	([abstract](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22684)). On a desktop card the CPU and GPU have
    70	separate memory; here every GPU byte competes with the CPU prover for the same 273 GB/s.
    71	ESTIMATE for MSM: a 4090 does a 2^20 BLS12-381 MSM in ~15-25 ms (sppark class); GB10 at
    72	~0.35× the int32 throughput → 50-80 ms; BLST on these 20 ARM cores ≈ 0.4-0.8 s → MSM gain
    73	5-10×. At a 30% MSM share (C0 owner `commitment`, phases 9, 15, 18, 20, 21):
    74	1/(0.7 + 0.3/7) = **1.35×**; at 50%: 1/(0.5 + 0.5/7) = 1.75×. NTT/quotient on GPU:
    75	2-4× on the stage at best because of the shared bus (ESTIMATE, no GB10 measurement found).
    76	Unified memory removes sppark's per-call copy weakness noted by Argon, but every GPU
    77	allocation counts against the same per-process 48 GB and aggregate 100 GB caps.
